  "live_records": {
    "q-166070": {
      "dom_id": "question-166070",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The function $p(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ g(x), & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind the value of the constant $k$ that makes $p(x)$ continuous at $x=3.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $k\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We have the piecewise function\n\n$$\np(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}k+10, & \\,x\\le 3, \\\\ 8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}, & \\,x>3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAt $x=3,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & =p(3) \\\\  & =k+10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8(3)-\\frac{2}{3}(3)^{3} \\\\  & =24-18 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $p(x)$ to be continuous at $x=3,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}p(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}p(x) \\\\ k+10 & =6 \\\\ k & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$ when $k=-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
